            <div class="topicContainer">
                <h2 id="world-Highlight">Introduction</h2>
                <p>
                    IronHammer started as a question:
                    <span id="world-Highlight"
                        >what does a game engine actually look like under the
                        hood?</span
                    >
                </p>
                <p>
                    I have experience working with commercial engines like Unity
                    and Unreal. While I can use them comfortably and work with
                    their built in systems, I always wanted to understand what
                    is actually happening beneath the surface. It is easy to
                    attach a collider component and have collision magically
                    work, but what is that magic, exactly? This project is my
                    attempt to find out by building those systems myself from
                    scratch.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The result is a complete 2D game engine and editor built
                    without any framework or scaffolding, only libraries for
                    windowing, graphics, and UI. Every architectural decision
                    had to be made deliberately, from how thousands of game
                    objects are stored in memory, to how the editor converts
                    between three different coordinate systems. The engine is
                    still actively in development. This page is a record of the
                    systems built so far and the thinking behind them.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="topicContainer">
                <h2 id="world-Highlight">Entity Component System</h2>
                <p>
                    The ECS is the foundation that everything else is built on.
                    Rather than storing game objects as individual classes
                    scattered across memory, IronHammer groups entities that
                    share the same set of components together in one contiguous
                    block, an <span id="world-Highlight">archetype</span>.
                    Within each archetype, all transforms are packed together,
                    all sprites are packed together, and so on.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This layout, known as
                    <span id="world-Highlight">Structure of Arrays</span>, means
                    that iterating 10,000 entities to move them is a single
                    sequential read through memory, rather than 10,000 random
                    jumps between objects. Modern CPUs are designed to handle
                    sequential reads efficiently, so the difference in
                    performance is significant and measurable.
                </p>

                <ul class="bulletPointList">
                    <li>
                        <b>Archetype Storage:</b> Entities sharing the same set
                        of components are grouped into a single contiguous
                        memory block. Adding or removing a component moves an
                        entity to the correct group automatically.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <b>Component Self Registration:</b> Every component
                        registers itself with the engine before the program
                        starts via a
                        <span id="world-Highlight">REGISTER_COMPONENT</span>
                        macro. This lets the engine construct, destroy, save,
                        and display any component through a uniform interface,
                        without knowing the specific type at the call site.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <b>Query System:</b> Systems declare what components
                        they need upfront. The engine pre filters matching
                        entity groups at startup, so at runtime there is no
                        searching, just a flat loop over pre validated data.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <b>Deferred Changes (CommandBuffer):</b> Structural
                        changes like adding or removing components are queued
                        during the frame and applied after all systems finish,
                        preventing the kind of mid loop corruption that would
                        otherwise be very difficult to debug.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <b>Generation Safety:</b> Every entity carries a
                        generation counter alongside its ID. When an entity is
                        destroyed and its slot is reused, the counter
                        increments. Any old reference to that entity will fail
                        validation automatically, no dangling pointers, no
                        silent bugs.
                    </li>
                </ul>

