            <div class="topicContainer">
                <h2 id="world-Highlight">Custom Memory Allocators</h2>
                <p>
                    Most programs rely on the operating system to hand out and
                    reclaim memory, a process that works well in general but has
                    measurable overhead when done thousands of times per frame.
                    IronHammer implements three purpose built allocators that
                    pre claim large blocks of memory up front and manage them
                    internally, keeping allocations fast and predictable.
                </p>

                <ul class="bulletPointList">
                    <li>
                        <b>Templated Slab Allocator:</b> A type safe, template
                        driven allocator that manages a growable list of fixed
                        size slabs on the heap. Each slab holds
                        <code>SlabSize</code> objects of type <code>T</code> and
                        tracks free slots using a 64 bit integer bitset, finding
                        the next available slot reduces to a single CPU
                        instruction (<span id="world-Highlight"
                            >std::countr_zero</span
                        >). Supports both single object and full array
                        allocation. Deallocated memory is returned to its
                        originating slab and made immediately available for
                        reuse, keeping fragmentation minimal. When all existing
                        slabs are full, a new one is appended automatically.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <b>Type Erased Slab Allocator:</b> Functionally similar
                        to the Templated Slab Allocator but operates without
                        compile time type information. Rather than being
                        dependent on a type, it accepts an element size at
                        construction and allocates raw 64 byte aligned memory
                        blocks accordingly. A linked free list is added directly
                        into the slab slots themselves, so no external
                        bookkeeping structure is needed. Typed access is
                        restored at the call site via reinterprate casting.
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <b>Type Erased Block Allocator:</b> A simpler, array
                        only variant of the type erased approach designed
                        specifically for archetype component storage. Rather
                        than allocating individual objects, it hands out entire
                        contiguous blocks of memory, one block per component
                        array per chunk. Individual element removal is handled
                        not by deallocation but by a swap and pop: the removed
                        element is overwritten by the last element in the array,
                        keeping the block fully packed at all times. Once enough
                        removals have accumulated and a block becomes empty, it
                        is returned to the free list and recycled for future
                        chunks.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <ul class="visualDemo">
                    <li>
                        <div>
                            <h3 id="world-Highlight">
                                Templated Slab Allocator
                            </h3>
                            <pre><code class="language-cpp">
// Free slots tracked as bits: 1 = free, 0 = used.
size_t FindFirstFreeBit()
{
    if constexpr (wordCount == 1)
    {
        if (bitSet.single != 0)
        {
            return std::countr_zero(bitSet.single);
        }

    }
    else
    {
        for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; wordCount; ++i)
        {
            if (bitSet.multiple[i] != 0)
            {
                return (i * 64) + std::countr_zero(bitSet.multiple[i]);
            }
        }
    }
}

T* Allocate()
{
    // Find or create a slab with space
    if (currentSlabIndex == SIZE_MAX || slabs[currentSlabIndex].freeCount == 0)
    {
        currentSlabIndex = FindFreeSlab();
        if (currentSlabIndex == SIZE_MAX)
        {
            slabs.emplace_back();
            currentSlabIndex = slabs.size() - 1;
        }
    }

    auto&  slab      = slabs[currentSlabIndex];
    size_t freeIndex = slab.FindFirstFreeBit();
    slab.SetBitToZero(freeIndex);
    --slab.freeCount;
    return &slab.blocks[freeIndex];
}

void DeAllocate(T* ptr)
{
    auto [slabIndex, blockIndex] = LocateBlock(ptr);
    slabs[slabIndex].ResetBitToOne(blockIndex);
    ++slabs[slabIndex].freeCount;
    currentSlabIndex = slabIndex;
}
</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <div>
                            <h3 id="world-Highlight">
                                Type Erased Slab Allocator
                            </h3>
                            <pre><code class="language-cpp">
struct Slot
{
    Slot* nextFreeSlot;       // free list pointer
    alignas(64) char data[1]; // actual object data lives here
};

void* InternalAllocate()
{
    if (m_freeList == nullptr) AllocateNewSlab();

    Slot* slot = m_freeList;
    m_freeList = m_freeList-&gt;nextFreeSlot; // pop from free list
    return slot-&gt;data;
}

void InternalDeallocate(void* ptr)
{
    // Recover the Slot header from the data pointer
    Slot* slot = reinterpret_cast&lt;Slot*&gt;(
        reinterpret_cast&lt;char*&gt;(ptr) - offsetof(Slot, data));

    slot-&gt;nextFreeSlot = m_freeList;  // push back onto free list
    m_freeList = slot;
}

template &lt;typename T, typename... Args&gt;
T* Allocate(Args&amp;&amp;... args)
{
    void* memory = InternalAllocate();
    return new (memory) T(std::forward&lt;Args&gt;(args)...);
}

template &lt;typename T&gt;
void Deallocate(T* obj)
{
    obj-&gt;~T();
    InternalDeallocate(obj);
}

</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <div>
                            <h3 id="world-Highlight">
                                Type Erased Block Allocator
                            </h3>
                            <pre><code class="language-cpp">
// Allocates entire contiguous blocks, one per component array per chunk.
// Blocks are 64 byte aligned so arrays land exactly on cache line boundaries.
void AllocateNewBlock()
{
    void* newBlock = operator new(
        m_dataPerBlock * m_dataSize,
        std::align_val_t(64)    // cache line aligned
    );
    m_allBlocks.push_back(newBlock);
    m_freeBlocks.push_back(newBlock);
}

void* AllocateBlock()
{
    if (m_freeBlocks.empty()) AllocateNewBlock();

    void* freeBlock = m_freeBlocks.back();
    m_freeBlocks.pop_back();
    return freeBlock;
}

void DeallocateBlock(void* ptr)
{
    m_freeBlocks.push_back(ptr);
}
</code></pre>
