            <div class="topicContainer">
                <h2 id="world-Highlight">Rendering System</h2>
                <p>
                    At the beginning of this project the one area where I felt
                    most lacking was graphics programming. Although I would love
                    to learn it from scratch, With all the other things that I
                    had to learn and implement, I decided to go with an open
                    source library to handle graphics. I'm already thinking
                    about upgrading the engine to support 3D graphics at some
                    point, that will be the time where I certainly try to make
                    my own libraries tailored to the engine. However, that's out
                    of scope currently. Therefore, I've decided to use SFML's
                    graphics module which is built on top of OpenGL. This will
                    allow simple rendering of sprites, shapes, lines and texts.
                    The key point is to ensure efficient rendering when entity
                    count grows and draw calls increase. The rendering system
                    avoids issuing one per entity by accumulating all geometry
                    into a single
                    <span id="world-Highlight">vertex array</span> and flushing
                    it in one call, or as few calls as possible. For sprites, a
                    new flush only happens when the texture changes. For shapes
                    and collider wireframes, flushes are triggered by a
                    configurable vertex budget.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Sprite geometry is computed manually per entity: rotation is
                    applied using a precomputed cosine/sine pair, UV coordinates
                    are mapped from each sprite's texture rectangle, and the
                    result is written directly into the batch. No intermediate
                    objects, no allocations per frame.
                </p>

